FujiNet Go MSX (desktop) -- what is done and what is next
===========================================================

The port follows fujinet-go-adam-desktop/PORTING.md, whose milestone order
(section 12) this file adapts. The derivation is

    msx-desktop = (adam-desktop - adam-android) + msx-android

with the CoCo port as the closer model for the "FujiNet listens, emulator
connects out" transport direction and the variable-frame-geometry pattern,
and ADAM as the closer model for the debugger engine (the MSX1 VDP is
literally a TMS9918A, so ADAM's vdpview.c is the widened base rather than a
reference).

MSX diverges from every sibling in one structural way the other three don't:
openMSX is not staged and compiled by this project's own CMake the way
adamcore/AppleWin/XRoar are. It is 570 C++23 translation units with a real
3rd-party dependency chain (Tcl, SDL2, SDL2_ttf, freetype, libpng, zlib,
ogg/vorbis/theora, GLEW), and openMSX's own GNUmakefile + build/3rdparty
chain already solves that per-platform -- the same chain the Android sibling
already drives successfully. cmake/StageOpenMSX.cmake stages + patches the
source, then invokes that chain rather than reinventing it. See
COMPLIANCE.md for the patch list.

Two more MSX-specific decisions, both settled before any code was written:
  * openMSX owns its own SDL2 Mixer audio and its own msxjoystick1/2 plugs.
    This app links no SDL3 at all -- core/src/audio_sdl.c and
    core/src/gamepad_sdl.c from the sibling repos are NOT ported.
  * Frame capture cannot use the Android app's -Wl,--wrap,SDL_GL_SwapWindow
    + glReadPixels trick (GNU ld/lld only, absent on macOS). The desktop tap
    is a hook in PostProcessor::rotateFrames() (src/video/PostProcessor.cc),
    openMSX's own per-frame CPU-side consumer of the finished FrameSource,
    reusing the getScaledFrame() helper already in that file. No GL readback
    at all.

Ports, fixed for this target (never 1985/1986/8055, which the Android
sibling and a standalone fujinet-pc-msx already use):
  BoIP (FujiBus over TCP, FujiNet listens, openMSX connects out): 65505
  Web UI:                                                         64003


Done (this pass) -- M0, M1
--------------------------

M1
  * cmake/Dependencies.cmake -- msx_provide_dependency(), ported from
    CoCo's coco_provide_dependency() and simplified: openMSX and
    fujinet-firmware are both live git forks with real history (unlike
    CoCo's XRoar tarball), so both resolve via the SRC-override / clone-at-
    pin path, with no .gitmodules registration (matches what CoCo's
    third_party/ actually does at runtime, despite its own comment saying
    "submodule" -- there is no .gitmodules there either). openMSX pinned at
    b11b550120fe9d342f9bd64128de7bd06e1a7758 (feat/fujinet), fujinet-firmware
    at b9a27cfa145134f5a7088535ac2721eb3cdf41be.
  * cmake/StageOpenMSX.cmake -- stages src/, build/, share/ and
    Contrib/cbios into core/openmsx-generated/, then runs
    tools/openmsx/patches/patch-staged-tree.py. Re-stages on source or
    patch-script change, or -DOPENMSX_RESTAGE=ON.
  * tools/openmsx/patches/patch-staged-tree.py -- two patches, both
    anchor-verified and confirmed idempotent by running twice against the
    real checkout:
      - src/serial/FujiNet.cc: FUJINET_DEFAULT_PORT 1985 -> 65505.
      - src/video/PostProcessor.cc: a msxhost_notify_frame(paintFrame) call
        inserted in rotateFrames(), right beside openMSX's own AVI-recorder
        tap (proof this is the right per-frame CPU-side hook point) --
        forward-declares FrameSource and the extern "C" hook once, near the
        top of the file.
  * cmake/OpenMSXRuntime.cmake + tools/openmsx/build-openmsx-desktop.sh --
    drives openMSX's own GNUmakefile over the staged+patched tree via bash
    (never a bare .sh), tolerates the expected final-executable-link
    failure (msxhost_notify_frame is undefined until M2 supplies it, same
    as the Android sibling tolerates its own link failure for a different
    reason), archives every object except main.o into libopenmsx.a, and
    writes include-dirs.txt from a `find src -type d` that mirrors
    openMSX's own SOURCE_DIRS logic (build/main.mk:260) so a future
    openMSX version adding a subdirectory doesn't silently break the
    include path. Linux links system Tcl/SDL2/freetype/etc dynamically
    (LINK_MODE=SYS_DYN, the default); darwin/mingw-w64 will additionally
    run `make 3rdparty` first for static linking (untested so far -- no
    Mac/Windows box; M7/M9 concern).
  * cmake/toolchains/mingw-w64.cmake -- ported from CoCo, scoped to
    cross-compiling the *session/frontend* C/C++ code, not openMSX itself
    (which needs a real MSYS2/UCRT64 shell for its own 3rdparty chain --
    windows.yml in CI builds natively, matching the family convention).
  * cmake/FujiNetRuntime.cmake -- ported verbatim from CoCo (renamed),
    unexercised until M3 (WITH_FUJINET=OFF so far).
  * CMakeLists.txt, core/CMakeLists.txt -- skeleton; core/tests/
    openmsx_link_test.cpp links openmsx-lib and calls the real
    openmsx::Version::full() (deliberately not just an empty translation
    unit against the headers, so a link-only build tells the real story).

  VERIFIED end to end, twice:
    1. Against the local OPENMSX_SRC=~/Workspace/openMSX checkout:
       staged, patched (both patches applied and confirmed idempotent),
       571 objects compiled, archived into libopenmsx.a, our own
       openmsx_link_test linked and ran ("openMSX 21.0-unknown" -- the
       "unknown" is expected, the staged tree has no .git metadata, same
       known condition PORTING.md documents for FujiNet's
       version_common.py -- not a defect here).
    2. The actual clean-clone gate: a fresh `git clone --local` of this
       repository (no OPENMSX_SRC override), submodule-init step failing
       gracefully as expected (no .gitmodules) and falling through to the
       clone-at-pin path, which checked out EXACTLY
       b11b550120fe9d342f9bd64128de7bd06e1a7758 (confirmed with git
       rev-parse), then staged/patched/compiled/archived/linked
       identically to run 1. (The clone was pointed at the local openMSX
       checkout's path rather than GitHub, purely to avoid a slow network
       fetch of the real ~580MB history during verification -- the code
       path exercised, and the pin match confirmed, are the real thing;
       only the URL was swapped for speed, and only in a throwaway
       scratch clone, never committed.)


Done (this pass) -- M0
-----------------------

  * git init, LICENSE (GPL-3.0, verbatim from fujinet-go-coco-desktop),
    .gitignore (build*/ with !build-aux/, core/openmsx-generated/,
    third_party/{openmsx,fujinet-firmware}/, tools/*/work/,
    .flatpak-builder/).
  * README.md -- family framing, planned-features list, MSX system ROM
    story (C-BIOS bundled unconditionally -- this is the one target in the
    family that gets to have that), status table (all four frontends "not
    started"/"in progress").
  * COMPLIANCE.md -- full per-component table, and in particular:
      - The openMSX licence determination (GPL-2.0-or-later, matching the
        Android sibling's call on the same ambiguous README wording) is
        written as a determination, not a quotation.
      - The three openMSX staged-source patches (BoIP port, the
        rotateFrames() frame hook, and the explicit *omission* of the
        Android sibling's touch-keyboard EventDelay patch -- a physical
        keyboard needs no release-delay hack).
      - C-BIOS bundled / turboR-and-real-machine ROMs never bundled, same
        enforcement shape as CoCo's no_embedded_roms.py (adapted: asserts
        absence rather than gating an ON/OFF option, since MSX has nothing
        to gate -- C-BIOS is always in).
  * No PORTING.md in this repo -- confirmed neither coco-desktop nor
    apple2-desktop carry their own copy either; only adam-desktop (the
    model repo) has one, and this file (TODO) is where the other three
    repos actually record per-repo decisions and status. README points at
    adam-desktop's PORTING.md for the shared specification.
  * GitHub repo NOT created/pushed yet -- local commits only until the
    maintainer says otherwise.


Done (this pass) -- M2
----------------------

  * core/openmsx/msx_host.{cc,hh} -- ported from the Android sibling's
    msx_host.cpp, Android-only halves dropped (no audio ring, no
    -Wl,--wrap trick, no JNI -- see the M2 commit message and
    COMPLIANCE.md). Reactor boot (-machine <id> -ext FujiNet, powerOn,
    run on its own thread) with a real success/failure handshake back to
    the caller (msxhost_core_start() blocks until known, unlike the
    Android host's fire-and-forget spawn). SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen
    headless GL context on Linux; macOS/Windows headless GL are still
    untested (no Mac/Windows box -- M7/M9). msxhost_notify_frame() packs
    the FrameSource into XRGB8888 via getLinePtr640_480() only -- see
    below, this ended up simpler than originally planned.
  * core/src/session.c + core/include/msxsession.h -- thin lifecycle
    wrapper (no phase-locked pacer: openMSX paces itself, `throttle on` +
    `fullspeedwhenloading off` so FujiNet's M3 "loading" hold won't race
    emulated time), the latest-frame double-buffer, key/joystick
    passthrough. msxsession_notify_vsync() is advisory only
    (MSX_PACE_LOG=1 diagnostic). No audio API at all -- openMSX owns its
    own SDL2 Mixer straight to the real device; this app links no SDL3
    (confirmed: audio_sdl.c/gamepad_sdl.c from the sibling repos are NOT
    ported, exactly as planned).
  * core/src/input_map.c -- msx_key_from_event(), X11 keysym ->
    SDL_Keycode + mod bits. No MSX keyboard-matrix table needed: openMSX's
    own Keyboard::processKeyEvent does that translation already.
  * frontends/gnome/{main,window,display}.c -- window + display + keyboard
    capture, Reset action, F11 fullscreen. M2 scope only: no menu beyond
    Reset/About (media import, machine switching, FujiNet config,
    debugger, preferences all land in M3-M6).
  * cmake/OpenMSXRuntime.cmake -- Linux dynamic-link deps (SDL2,
    SDL2_ttf, libpng, ogg/theora/vorbis, GLEW, zlib, GL, ALSA, freetype,
    Tcl) added, matching `ldd` on a native openMSX build exactly.

  CORRECTED from the original M2 plan (both discovered by testing, not
  by re-reading the plan):
    1. msxhost_notify_frame() only needs getLinePtr640_480() -- not a
       getLinePtr320_240()-vs-640_480() choice keyed off the source
       resolution. That API scales *any* source frame to a fixed
       640x480 unconditionally (it exists for exactly this purpose --
       video recording), so msxsession's frame geometry is FIXED, not
       variable like the sibling targets. This simplified
       msxsession_copy_frame() (no w_out/h_out) and display.c (no
       per-frame texture resize) versus what was planned.
    2. The plan predicted "openMSX's own executable link now succeeds"
       once msxhost_notify_frame is defined. This was wrong and stays
       wrong forever, not just for M2: that symbol is defined in
       msx_host.cc, which is part of libmsxsession, and is NEVER linked
       into openMSX's own derived/<platform>/bin/openmsx executable (we
       only ever extract *objects* from that build, never link the
       executable itself). build-openmsx-desktop.sh's tolerated-failure
       branch is a permanent, expected feature of this port, not an
       M1-only condition -- every future milestone's build log will keep
       showing it, and that is correct.

  A REAL BUG caught by the clean-clone gate test (see the fix commit
  after M2's): cmake/OpenMSXRuntime.cmake computed msxsession's
  per-subdirectory openMSX include path from a file
  (tools/openmsx/work/out/include-dirs.txt) that only exists after
  build-openmsx-desktop.sh has already run once -- but a target's
  INTERFACE_INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES is baked in at CMake *generate* time,
  which happens once per configure and is never revisited just because a
  later build step writes a new file. On this development machine the
  bug was invisible for both the M1 and initial M2 verification passes,
  because build/ already had a populated include-dirs.txt left over from
  an earlier successful build in the same tree -- it only showed up when
  a genuine `git clone --local` into an *empty* directory was tried,
  which is exactly why that gate exists and is worth re-running for real
  after every milestone, not just trusted from memory. Fixed by
  computing the subdirectory list from the staged source tree directly
  (populated earlier in the same configure pass by StageOpenMSX.cmake)
  instead of a build-time artifact.

  VERIFIED end to end:
    - boot_smoke (new ctest): C-BIOS MSX2 boots headless, 30 real
      non-blank 640x480 frames in 1.45s.
    - The GNOME frontend launched under a real X11 display (DISPLAY=:1),
      ran 36s, no crash, no MSX-specific error (only this system's
      pre-existing GTK/libadwaita theme-CSS warnings, unrelated).
    - Both ctest cases pass from a fresh `rm -rf build` rebuild AND from
      a genuine `git clone --local` into an empty directory with no
      OPENMSX_SRC override (the include-path bug above was caught and
      fixed via exactly this second check).


Done (this pass) -- M3
----------------------

  * tools/fujinet/build-fujinet-desktop.sh + support/fujinet_desktop_
    entry.cpp -- ported from CoCo (~95% target-independent, confirmed by
    doing it): PC_TARGET=RS232 (fujinet-pc has no dedicated MSX target,
    matching the Android sibling), BOIP forced to 65505 matching what M1's
    patch-staged-tree.py already compiles into openMSX's own
    FUJINET_DEFAULT_PORT, web UI default bind 64003. RS232 has no
    BUILD_RS232/fnconfig.ini in the firmware tree (confirmed by reading
    the source tree -- only autorun.img, unlike COCO's dedicated one), so
    the build falls back to the generic dist config; force_boip_config()
    stamps the correct BOIP port over it regardless either way, so this
    is cosmetic (a possibly Atari-flavoured default host list) not a
    functional gap. Worth fixing in a later pass if it turns out to
    matter to users.
  * core/src/paths.c + dynlib.h -- ported from CoCo, paths.c trimmed (no
    ROM materialisation -- C-BIOS is bundled via openMSX's own share
    tree, not per-app embedded blobs). Wired msxsession.h's
    fujinet_runtime_src parameter through end to end -- it existed since
    M2 but was never actually connected to anything; fixed while this
    area was already being touched rather than leave known-dead API
    surface.
  * core/src/fujinet_runtime.c -- ported from CoCo with SAM speech-audio
    mixing dropped entirely (RS232 has no Atari-hardware SAM equivalent,
    and openMSX owns its own audio regardless). fujinet_wait_for_boip()
    keeps CoCo's connect-probe design -- confirmed by reading
    lib/hardware/BoIPChannel.cpp that it is a shared component between
    the RS232 and COCO/DriveWire buses, so the same "suspends up to 2s
    before listening" behaviour applies here too.
  * core/src/session.c -- msxsession_start() starts FujiNet first and
    waits for the BOIP listener before booting openMSX (same ordering as
    CoCo); msxsession_stop() stops it after.
  * core/tests/fujinet_smoke.c (new ctest) -- boots openMSX with FujiNet
    enabled and confirms "BoIPChannel: connection from:" appears in the
    FujiNet console log. Confirmed by reading
    lib/hardware/BoIPChannel.cpp's accept_connection(): this line is
    logged by the *listener* side on accept, so it is genuine proof
    openMSX's FujiNet device connected in, not just that something
    answered a probe. Exits 77 (ctest SKIP) when FujiNet isn't running,
    matching boot_smoke's ROM-less-build skip shape.

  CORRECTED from the original M3 plan: the BOIP connection does not wait
  for the 'c' keypress at all -- openMSX's FujiNet device connects out as
  soon as the FujiNet extension is inserted at boot, before C-BIOS's boot
  menu is even up. fujinet_smoke still injects 'c' as a closer-to-real-
  usage check, but the connection it actually looks for in the log
  happens earlier and is not gated on it.

  A SECOND real bug caught by the clean-clone gate test in this same
  milestone (see the fix commit after M3's main one): core/CMakeLists.txt
  gated fujinet_smoke's registration on `if(WITH_FUJINET)`, but that
  option is declared inside cmake/FujiNetRuntime.cmake, which the
  top-level CMakeLists.txt includes *after* add_subdirectory(core) --
  so the variable did not exist yet at that point and read as
  unset/false, silently skipping the test's registration even on a
  WITH_FUJINET=ON build. The FujiNet runtime itself still built
  successfully (FujiNetRuntime.cmake's own option() call ran later in the
  same pass and defaulted on), so nothing about the build looked broken;
  ctest just quietly ran two tests instead of three. CoCo's sibling
  avoids this whole class of bug by never gating its own FujiNet-
  dependent test (drivewire_smoke) on WITH_FUJINET at all -- fixed the
  same way: register unconditionally, skip (77) at runtime instead. This
  is now the SECOND time in this repo's history that the clean-clone gate
  test (not a plain in-place rebuild) caught a real bug M1 and M2's own
  "it works" verification had missed -- worth remembering as a reason to
  keep running it every milestone, not just early on.

  VERIFIED end to end, including after the WITH_FUJINET fix: all three
  ctest cases pass, both from a rebuild in place and from a genuine fresh
  `git clone --local` with no *_SRC overrides. fujinet_smoke's own run
  shows the real sequence -- FujiNet listens on 127.0.0.1:65505, openMSX's
  FujiNet device connects in immediately at extension-boot time,
  "BoIPChannel: connection from: 127.0.0.1" logged, clean shutdown, exit 0.


Done (this pass) -- M4
----------------------

  * core/src/settings.c -- ported verbatim from CoCo (renamed). machine
    and enable_fujinet now persist across runs (msxsession_default_opts
    reads them instead of hardcoding C-BIOS MSX2 / fujinet-on).
  * core/openmsx/msx_host.{hh,cc} -- msxhost_switch_machine(): a second
    cross-thread queue (parallel to the existing input-event one, drained
    the same way from the frame hook) running openMSX's own Tcl
    "machine <id>" (Reactor::switchMachine(), confirmed by reading
    src/Reactor.cc's MachineCommand -- genuinely built into openMSX for
    live switching) followed by "ext FujiNet" (confirmed by reading
    MSXMotherBoard.cc's ExtCmd that a machine switch rebuilds the whole
    board and does NOT carry the old one's extensions over -- this would
    have been a silent "FujiNet stops working after switching machines"
    bug if missed). Machine ids are validated against a safe-character
    set before being interpolated into the Tcl command string.
  * core/src/session.c -- msxsession_set_machine()/machine() (persist +
    live-switch-if-running), msxsession_import_media() (.dsk/.cas ->
    FujiNet SD, .rom -> carts_dir) and msxsession_import_rom() (->
    roms_dir), ported from CoCo's pattern.
  * core/tests/machine_switch_test.c (new ctest) -- the real proof: boots
    MSX2, live-switches to MSX1 with the session staying "running"
    throughout, confirms frames keep flowing post-switch (no wedge), and
    confirms a FRESH BOIP connection appears after the switch (not just
    the old one lingering) -- i.e. "ext FujiNet" genuinely landed on the
    new board.
  * frontends/gnome/window.c -- Machine radio submenu (live switching),
    Import Disk/Cassette/Cartridge…, FujiNet Configuration… (system
    browser via GtkUriLauncher -- no embedded WebKitGTK yet, M5+),
    FujiNet Console Log… (plain GtkTextView dialog).

  SCOPE CUT, documented rather than silently dropped: real-gamepad-to-
  msxjoystick binding (letting a physical SDL gamepad drive
  msxjoystick1/2 instead of the synthetic on-screen/keyboard-driven
  ports) is NOT done. openMSX auto-detects real joysticks itself (it
  owns SDL2), but the msxjoystick1_config/msxjoystick2_config Tcl
  bindings msx_host.cc sets at boot only recognise the synthetic
  "joy1"/"joy2" ids -- a real pad plugged into the host is not bound to
  anything today. This needs UI to let the user pick which pad drives
  which port (mirroring the sibling repos' gamepad_sdl.c layer design)
  more than it needs new session/host plumbing, so it is deferred rather
  than half-built. Revisit alongside M5 (KDE) or as its own pass.

  msxsession_import_rom() (turboR/real-machine ROM -> roms_dir) is built
  and tested via the generic copy_file() path it shares with
  import_media, but deliberately NOT wired into the GNOME menu: openMSX
  needs a machine-config XML matching the real machine's hardwareconfig
  to actually boot from an imported ROM, and this milestone does not
  build that generator. Exposing "Import System ROM…" in the UI now
  would suggest a capability that does not exist yet.

  VERIFIED end to end: all 4 ctest cases pass, both from a rebuild in
  place and from a genuine fresh `git clone --local` with no *_SRC
  overrides -- clean this time, no third clean-clone-only bug.
  machine_switch_test's own run: MSX2 boots (serial=1), live switch to
  MSX1 (serial=32 after, frames kept flowing), FujiNet's old connection
  drops and a fresh one lands within ~100ms of the switch. The GNOME
  frontend launched under a live X11 display with the new menu wired in
  (FujiNet now starts automatically, enable_fujinet defaulting to 1 via
  settings) and the console log showed REAL rs232Fuji command traffic
  (GET WIFI STATUS, READ HOST SLOTS, READ DEVICE SLOTS) -- proof the MSX
  software stack itself is talking to FujiNet over the real protocol,
  not just a socket-level probe. No crash; only this system's
  pre-existing GTK theme-CSS warnings, unrelated to this code.


Done (this pass) -- M5
----------------------

  * frontends/kde/{DisplayWidget,MainWindow,FujiNetWindows,main}.{h,cpp}
    -- ported from CoCo's KDE frontend, adapted the same two ways the
    GNOME frontend already was: DisplayWidget's frame buffer is fixed
    640x480 (no per-mode aspect table), and MainWindow's Machine menu
    switches live via msxsession_set_machine() rather than doing a hard
    reset. Key mapping (Qt key code -> X11 keysym) uses the exact same
    XK_* numeric values core/src/input_map.c hardcodes, kept in sync
    deliberately between the two frontends.
  * frontends/kde/CMakeLists.txt -- no enable_language(CXX) or
    CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD override needed, unlike CoCo's: this project
    already enables C++23 project-wide for core/openmsx/msx_host.cc, so
    the KDE frontend just inherits it.
  * Same scope cut as GNOME's, restated here rather than silently
    dropped: no Import System ROM menu item (same reasoning -- no
    machine-config generator yet to make an imported ROM bootable).

  VERIFIED: clean build against Qt6 6.11.1 (pkg-config on this
  machine) on the first attempt -- no compile errors at all, the only
  surprises this pass were expected clangd noise before reconfiguring.
  A live run under a real X11 display showed the identical success
  pattern the GNOME frontend's own verification showed: FujiNet
  started, BOIP connected, and the console log carried real rs232Fuji
  command traffic (GET WIFI STATUS, READ HOST SLOTS, READ DEVICE
  SLOTS) -- this time reached through the Qt display widget instead of
  GTK's. No crash, no Qt warnings (notably quieter than GTK's
  pre-existing theme-CSS noise on this system). All 4 ctest cases pass,
  both rebuilt in place and from a genuine fresh `git clone --local`
  with no *_SRC overrides.


Done (this pass) -- M6
----------------------

Debugger engine + the V9938/V9958 VDP debugger + the GNOME debugger
window. The largest single milestone; read this in full before
continuing.

Foundation built and VERIFIED first, before any debugger.c/VDP code, since
everything else in this milestone depends on it:

  * core/openmsx/msx_host.{hh,cc} -- msxhost_execute_sync(command,
    result_out, result_max): runs an arbitrary openMSX Tcl command ON the
    openMSX thread and blocks the caller until it has actually run,
    returning the Tcl result (or exception message) as text. This is the
    one primitive the whole debugger is built on: "debug break"/"step"/
    "cont" (which Debugger.cc forwards straight to
    MSXCPUInterface::doBreak/doStep/doContinue -- confirmed by reading the
    source rather than assuming), "debug set_bp"/"remove_bp"/"list_bp",
    the "reg"/"cpuregs" Tcl procs already in share/scripts/_cpuregs.tcl,
    and "debug read_block". DEVIATES from the original plan text (which
    described pause/step/continue calling doBreak/doStep/doContinue
    directly in C++): decided instead to route everything, including
    those three, through this one already-proven Tcl RPC rather than add
    a second, narrower cross-thread primitive next to it -- simpler, and
    the overhead of one more Tcl command parse is irrelevant next to
    human reaction time.
  * A THIRD staged-source patch (tools/openmsx/patches/patch-staged-
    tree.py, patch_debug_pump_hook()): src/Reactor.cc gets a
    msxhost_debug_pump() call at the top of every Reactor::run() loop
    iteration, unconditionally (running or blocked). Necessary because
    the existing per-frame hook (msxhost_notify_frame(), in
    PostProcessor::rotateFrames()) stops firing entirely the instant
    "debug break" halts the CPU -- confirmed by reading Reactor::run()
    itself: eventDistributor->deliverEvents() runs every iteration
    regardless of blocked state, so hooking there keeps the command queue
    and the RPC draining at ~20ms cadence even while broken. Without this
    patch, every debugger control issued while stopped would hang for
    msxhost_execute_sync's own 5s timeout.
  * msxhost_execute_sync_binary(command, result_out, result_max,
    result_len_out) -- a SECOND RPC primitive, binary-safe, added after
    reasoning through (not after hitting) a real correctness trap: Tcl's
    TclObject::getString() forces a bytearray through its UTF-8-ish
    *string* representation, where byte values above 0x7F expand to
    multi-byte sequences and 0x00 truncates a C string outright. VDP
    registers, palette entries and VRAM contents live in exactly that
    0x00-0xFF range, so "debug read_block ..." results MUST go through
    this path (TclObject::getBinary(), a std::span<const uint8_t> over
    the Tcl_Obj's real byte-array storage) and never through
    msxhost_execute_sync's %s/text path.
  * core/tests/rpc_test.c (new ctest) -- exercises msxhost_execute_sync
    directly: a trivial command while running, a register read, "debug
    break" followed by TWO commands issued while genuinely broken (the
    case the third patch exists for), "debug cont" with confirmed frame
    resumption, and a msxhost_execute_sync_binary round-trip of a poked
    0xAB byte through "debug read_block VRAM 0 1" (would have come back
    corrupted through the text path -- this is what proves the binary
    primitive is actually needed, not just defensive).

  VERIFIED end to end: full rebuild after re-staging openMSX from scratch
  (confirmed via grep that the third patch landed in the fresh
  Reactor.cc), all 5 ctest cases pass (the 4 from M1-M5 plus the new
  rpc_test), including the broken-CPU RPC case and the high-byte binary
  round-trip.

Second pass, all VERIFIED end to end against a genuine from-scratch
rebuild (rm -rf core/openmsx-generated tools/openmsx/work build; a fresh
third_party/fujinet-firmware clone happened too, since the FUJINET_SRC
override was only ever a build-dir cache value) plus ctest:

  * core/debugger/z80dasm.{c,h} -- ported verbatim from adam-desktop
    (ADAMDASM_ -> MSXDASM_, #include "adamdebug.h" -> "msxdebug.h", no
    other changes -- same CPU). core/tests/dasm_test.c ported the same
    way (same golden-file corpus).
  * core/debugger/symbols.{c,h} -- ported verbatim (generic .sym-format
    parser, nothing ADAM-specific was in it to rename beyond the header
    guard and the builtin-table extern).
  * core/debugger/symbols_builtin.c -- NOT a port (ADAM's was machine-
    generated from a verified EOS/OS7 disassembly this project has no
    equivalent of). Deliberately SMALL and conservative rather than a
    guessed-at full BIOS jump table: the VDP/PSG/cassette/keyboard/
    printer I/O block (WRTVDP $47 through LPTSTT $A8, internally self-
    consistent -- every entry is a 3-byte JP slot at a fixed stride, NMI
    at $66 skipped since that address is CPU-wired not BIOS-assigned) plus
    the two best-known interrupt hooks (H.KEYI $FD9A, H.TIMI $FD9F). The
    slot-primitive block (RDSLT/WRSLT/CALSLT/ENASLT/CALLF) is NOT included
    -- addresses recalled with lower confidence, left to
    msxdebug_symbols_load() from a verified external table instead of
    risking wrong "facts" in a shipped product.
  * core/include/msxdebug.h + core/debugger/debugger.c -- the engine,
    built entirely on msxhost_execute_sync/_binary (M6 part 1's RPC), NOT
    on an exec hook (openMSX has none exposed to us, and one Tcl round
    trip per instruction is many orders of magnitude too slow to trace at
    full CPU speed -- so unlike ADAM, this engine has NO instruction-trace
    ring; scope cut, stated plainly, same as M4's gamepad-binding cut).
    DEVIATES from the milestone's own original plan text (which said
    pause/step/continue would call doBreak/doStep/doContinue directly in
    C++): everything, including those three, goes through the one already-
    proven msxhost_execute_sync RPC instead -- simpler than adding a
    second narrower primitive, and irrelevant overhead next to human
    reaction time. Two ways a stop is discovered: synchronously inline
    (pause/step_into, which block for their own RPC and call the
    callback directly) or via a small poll thread the engine owns
    (persistent breakpoints and step_over/step_out/run_to's transient
    one-shot conditional breakpoints, none of which have a push
    notification over the Tcl interface). step_over/step_out use a
    one-shot "debug set_bp -once <target> {[reg SP] >=|> <floor>}" at the
    return address -- the same SP-floor trick ADAM's exec hook used,
    expressed as a Tcl condition since openMSX has no native step-over.

    TWO REAL BUGS caught by core/tests/debugger_test.c (a new ctest
    exercising pause/step/disassembly/a real autonomous breakpoint against
    a live boot), both fixed before this pass closed:
      1. d->pending_reason relied on calloc-zero-init, but
         MSXDBG_STOP_PAUSE is enum value 0 -- so the very first autonomous
         breakpoint ever hit reported reason PAUSE instead of BREAKPOINT.
         Fixed: msxdebug_create() now explicitly initializes it to
         MSXDBG_STOP_BREAKPOINT.
      2. A genuine race in msxdebug_resume() (and the step_over/step_out/
         run_to helper): d->mtx was released, with paused set to 0, BEFORE
         "debug cont" was actually issued -- during that window the poll
         thread could observe paused==0, immediately check "debug
         breaked" (still true from whatever broke it BEFORE this resume),
         and misreport a stale stop at the OLD pc. debugger_test caught it
         directly: a breakpoint set several instructions ahead fired
         "immediately" at the pre-resume PC instead of the real target.
         Fixed by holding d->mtx across "debug cont" itself in all three
         places, not just the state update before it (safe: a different
         mutex than msxhost_execute_sync's own, no deadlock).
  * core/include/msxvdp.h + core/debugger/msxvdpview.c -- the VDP debugger,
    SCOPE STATED PLAINLY rather than silently narrowed from the original
    plan's "full V9938/V9958 widening": snapshot capture (via
    msxhost_execute_sync_binary against the "physical VRAM", "VDP regs"
    (0x40 bytes -- R32-R46 the command engine included, no separate
    source), "VDP status regs", "VDP palette" and "VRAM pointer"
    debuggables -- names confirmed by reading VDP.cc/VDPVRAM.cc directly,
    not assumed; NOTE the last one is bare "VRAM pointer", not "VDP VRAM
    pointer" as an earlier planning note in this file assumed), the full
    V9938/V9958 mode table (TEXT1/2, MULTICOLOR, GRAPHIC1-7, YJK/YAE),
    decoded via openMSX's OWN src/video/DisplayMode.hh bit arithmetic
    (read directly from that source while writing this, not reconstructed
    from memory -- exactly as authoritative as openMSX's own renderer),
    register/status/command-engine text description, a physical-VRAM hex
    dump, and palette-swatch rendering (V9938/58 palette RAM in RBG order,
    falling back to the fixed TMS9918A table on V9918). NOT done in this
    pass: nametable/pattern/sprite RASTER rendering for the V9938-only
    bitmap modes (Graphic 4-7), sprite mode 2, and YJK/YAE color decode --
    msxvdp_render_nametable/_patterns/_sprites render the MSX1-compatible
    modes (Graphic 1/2/3, Multicolor, Text 1) exactly as ADAM's vdpview.c
    validated, and fill a mid-gray placeholder for anything else rather
    than render garbage (the same honest-failure precedent
    ADAMVDP_MODE_INVALID set). Revisit as a follow-up pass; the register/
    status/palette/memory side a debugger session actually needs first is
    complete and tested.
  * core/tests/vdp_test.c (new ctest) -- a real snapshot against a live
    MSX2 boot decodes to a non-"undefined" mode, a VRAM poke via "debug
    write" round-trips through the snapshot's binary-safe capture and
    shows up correctly in the hex dump, and the palette renderer produces
    a non-uniform buffer.

Third pass -- the GNOME debugger window:

  * frontends/gnome/debugger/dbg_window.{c,h} -- ported from adam-desktop's
    dbg_window.c: disassembly with a click-to-toggle breakpoint gutter,
    editable registers (Enter applies while paused, byte-pair fields
    matching msxdebug_z80_regs' layout), a memory view, a breakpoint list,
    and the VDP tab (nametable/pattern/sprite/palette pictures, decoded
    register/status/command-engine text via msxvdp_format_state, and a
    physical-VRAM hex editor). F5 pause/continue, F7 step into, F8 step
    over, Shift+F8 step out -- same bindings as ADAM's. NOT ported: the
    Trace tab (msxdebug has no instruction-trace ring -- see the M6
    engine notes above; this is not an oversight, there is nothing for a
    trace tab to show).
  * frontends/gnome/window.c -- F12 (already reserved from forwarding to
    the MSX since M2) now opens the debugger; a "Debugger (F12)" menu
    entry added; MSX_OPEN_DEBUGGER=1 dev affordance opens it alongside the
    main window at startup (matching the sibling repos), MSX_DEBUGGER_TAB=
    vdp on the debugger window itself selects the starting tab.
  * VDP chip (V9918/V9938/V9958) is inferred once at debugger-window-open
    time from the current machine id (chip_from_machine()) rather than
    tracked live -- reopening the window after an M4 live machine switch
    picks up the new chip, judged an acceptable simplification over
    threading a live chip-change notification through for a debugging UI.

  VERIFIED end to end: full 8-test ctest suite (the 4 from M1-M5, rpc_test,
  and dasm_test/debugger_test/vdp_test) passes after forcing a completely
  fresh rebuild (re-staged openMSX, re-cloned fujinet-firmware, fresh
  build/ directory) -- not just an in-place rebuild. The GNOME frontend
  itself launched under a real X11 display with MSX_OPEN_DEBUGGER=1
  MSX_DEBUGGER_TAB=vdp: C-BIOS MSX2 booted, FujiNet connected (BoIPChannel
  accepted, real rs232Fuji traffic -- GET WIFI STATUS, READ HOST SLOTS,
  READ DEVICE SLOTS -- logged same as prior milestones), and the debugger
  window opened alongside it on the VDP tab with no crash and no
  debugger-specific GTK critical/error over a 20+ second run (only this
  system's pre-existing theme-CSS warnings, unrelated, same noise every
  milestone's GNOME verification has already shown). A screenshot tool
  was not available in this environment to additionally eyeball the
  rendered layout; functional correctness of every value the window
  displays is what ctest's debugger_test/vdp_test actually verify.

M6 is COMPLETE with two scope cuts stated plainly rather than silently
dropped, both documented above and worth restating here: (1) no
instruction-trace ring (architecturally not viable over a Tcl RPC at full
CPU speed, unlike ADAM's native exec hook), and (2) VDP raster rendering
(nametable/pattern/sprite pictures) covers only the MSX1-compatible modes
-- Graphic 4-7 bitmap modes, sprite mode 2, and YJK/YAE color decode are
not rendered as pictures yet (msxvdp_format_state's text decode covers
every mode; only the pictures are narrower). The register/status/palette/
memory/command-engine side of the VDP debugger -- the part a real
debugging session leans on most -- is complete for V9918/V9938/V9958
alike.


In progress -- M7
-----------------

CI workflows. Order per the plan: linux.yml first, then macos.yml and
windows.yml, then flatpak.yml.

  * .github/workflows/linux.yml -- installs openMSX's own Linux
    dependency list (tcl-dev, libsdl2-dev, libsdl2-ttf-dev, libpng-dev,
    libogg/vorbis/theora-dev, libglew-dev, zlib1g-dev, libfreetype-dev,
    libgl-dev, libasound2-dev -- matching README.md's documented list and
    cmake/OpenMSXRuntime.cmake's real `ldd` cross-check) plus GTK4/
    libadwaita and Qt6 for the two existing frontends, then a plain
    configure/build/ctest. No WITH_*_ROMS=OFF flag needed, unlike the
    sibling repos' linux.yml -- C-BIOS is bundled unconditionally (see
    COMPLIANCE.md), so this is the one FujiNet Go target whose CI boots
    real firmware rather than skipping ROM-gated tests. The package list
    and build flow are the same ones exercised, repeatedly, by every
    from-scratch local rebuild this session already ran -- the highest-
    confidence of the three OS workflows.
  * .github/workflows/macos.yml, windows.yml -- written from the CoCo
    sibling's structure, adapted for openMSX's own static-3rdparty build
    chain (NEED_3RDPARTY in tools/openmsx/build-openmsx-desktop.sh,
    triggered by OPENMSX_TARGET_OS=darwin/mingw-w64) rather than a
    separate pinned-SDL3 build step. EXPLICITLY UNVERIFIED: there is no
    Mac or Windows machine in this development environment, so neither
    openMSX's darwin/mingw-w64 3rd-party chain nor msx_host.cc's
    SDL_VIDEODRIVER=offscreen headless-GL hint (used unconditionally,
    on every platform -- no macOS/Windows-specific branch was written,
    since "offscreen" is a portable SDL2 driver rather than an X11/EGL-
    specific one, and adding an unverifiable speculative branch seemed
    worse than flagging the untested case honestly) has ever actually
    run outside Linux. These two workflow files are a best-effort
    starting point; a real run on push is what actually tests them, same
    as M1's own "darwin/mingw-w64 will additionally run `make 3rdparty`
    first -- untested so far" note has said since the very first
    milestone.

  NOT yet done in M7: flatpak.yml, and the build-aux/flatpak/online.
  fujinet.go.msx.{gnome,kde}.yml manifests it depends on (which do not
  exist yet -- unlike the CI workflows above, a flatpak manifest is a
  from-scratch artifact needing every dependency module (tcl, sdl2,
  sdl2_ttf, freetype, libpng, glew, libogg, libvorbis, libtheora, zlib,
  plus FujiNet's own mbedtls/libssh/libsmb2/libnfs/expat/cjson) declared
  with correct sources and hashes -- real, separate work, deliberately
  not rushed alongside the three CI workflows above. Next concrete step
  for M7.

